--TEST--
`as object`, `as callable` and `as Core\Namespace` from anything that is not already an object are `E0711`, `rule:types/conversion`'s table naming no row that produces one
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:types/conversion` tabulates no conversion into an object. A class target is
// admitted anyway because it can be checked — the value's runtime class is
// compared against a descriptor — and that holds for a declared class and for
// a `Core` class with instances alike, the second against the descriptor the
// process publishes. These three name no descriptor at all, so the conversion
// could only assert the tag it cannot verify.
mixed $m = 3;
object $o = $m as object;
callable $c = $m as callable;
// A `Core` *namespace* class declares neither a property nor an instance
// member, so nothing is ever an instance of it and there is nothing to test.
$m as Core\Json;
// The operand's own type does not rescue it: `string` is no closer to an
// object than `mixed` is, it just knows so at compile time.
string $s = "x";
object $p = $s as object;
